---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EdgeGlyph from '../../components/EdgeGlyph.astro';
import { EDGE_TYPES, type EdgeType } from '../../lib/graph';
import { LOCALES, t, type Lang } from '../../lib/i18n';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return LOCALES.map((lang) => ({ params: { lang } }));
}

const lang = Astro.params.lang as Lang;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const root = `${base}/${lang}`;
const REPO = 'https://github.com/TrDung22/3d-vision-genealogy';
const plainTitle = t(lang, 'about.title').replace(/<[^>]+>/g, '');
---

<Base title={t(lang, 'nav.about')} lang={lang} description={plainTitle}>
  <article class="about">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">{t(lang, 'about.kicker')}</p>
      <h1 set:html={t(lang, 'about.title')} />
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="dropcap">
        When I started learning 3D computer vision, I found myself with the two familiar
        options: read surveys (you get a taxonomy, you lose the story) or crawl
        awesome-lists (you get a warehouse, you lose the order). Both answer
        <em>"what exists"</em> — but neither answers the question a learner actually needs:
      </p>
      <blockquote class="pull">Why was this born, and what did it fix about what came before it?</blockquote>
      <p>
        Paper B exists because paper A failed at one specific spot. A direction gets
        abandoned by the whole community for decades, then suddenly comes back to life. Two
        groups who have never met touch the same idea in the same year. That causal texture
        is the part I kept losing — so this site records 3D vision as a <em>genealogy</em>:
        every work is a node, and every edge is an intellectual debt with an explicit name.
      </p>

      <h2>The vocabulary</h2>
      <ul class="vocab-list">
        {
          (Object.keys(EDGE_TYPES) as EdgeType[]).map((key) => (
            <li>
              <EdgeGlyph type={key} width={44} arrow={key !== 'independent'} />
              <span>
                <strong>{EDGE_TYPES[key].label}</strong> — {t(lang, `glossary.${key}`)}
              </span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        <em>Fixes</em> and <em>builds on</em> — every survey tells those stories. It's the
        last three that linear write-ups drop on the floor, and they are the soul of this
        project:
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="soul">
      <section class="card soul-card">
        <EdgeGlyph type="independent" width={56} />
        <h3>Independent</h3>
        <p>
          Occupancy Networks and DeepSDF touched the same idea — continuous implicit
          representations — at the same CVPR 2019, neither descending from the other (IM-Net
          and PIFu made it four). When an idea is ripe, it falls everywhere at once; the
          interesting question is <em>what conditions</em> made it ripe.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="card soul-card soul-challenges">
        <EdgeGlyph type="challenges" width={56} arrow />
        <h3>Challenges</h3>
        <p>
          In 2019, Tatarchenko and colleagues proved that single-view 3D reconstruction
          networks were actually doing… recognition in disguise: a simple retrieval baseline
          beat them all. Five years later, Banani et al. asked the same uncomfortable question
          of the foundation-model era. Progress is not just accumulation — some papers are
          great precisely because they show an entire branch has been fooling itself.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="card soul-card">
        <EdgeGlyph type="revives" width={56} arrow />
        <h3>Revives</h3>
        <p>
          Horn's shape-from-shading (1970) was forgotten for half a century, reawakened by
          SIRFS in 2012, then eclipsed again by the CNN wave. The "mainstream" is only one of
          many possible roads — and orphaned branches have a habit of returning when nobody
          expects them.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Where it starts</h2>
      <p>
        The first branch is <a href={`${root}/branches/single-image-3d/`}>single-image 3D</a>
        — reconstructing 3D from one photo, the original ill-posed problem of machine
        vision. I chose it because its history is a beautifully closed arc: every
        generation orbits a single question,
        <strong>"where does the prior come from?"</strong> Physical priors (Horn),
        statistical priors (Make3D), in-domain deep priors (Eigen), multi-dataset priors
        (MiDaS), representation priors (voxels → points → meshes → implicit functions), and
        finally internet-scale priors borrowed from image generators (Zero-1-to-3, LRM, and
        everything since).
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href={`${root}/`}>atlas</a> on the home page is the map of that story. Three
        more branches have since been grafted on — <a href={`${root}/branches/neural-rendering/`}>neural rendering</a>,
        <a href={`${root}/branches/multi-view-geometry/`}>multi-view geometry</a> and
        <a href={`${root}/branches/dynamic-4d/`}>dynamic scenes &amp; 4D</a> — and the data
        model keeps grafting as cheap as adding files. Each node will gradually thicken into
        its own analysis on the blog.
      </p>

      <h2>Editorial principles</h2>
      <ol class="principles">
        <li>Every node must answer three questions: <em>what problem — what idea — what limitations it left behind</em>.</li>
        <li>Every edge must point at the <em>specific weakness</em> the later work repaired — no hand-waving.</li>
        <li>Historical honesty over fame: a lightly-cited work that played its role still gets its place.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="contribute card">
        If you spot a missing node, a wrong relation, or an orphaned branch nobody has told
        — the <a href={REPO} target="_blank" rel="noopener">repo is open on GitHub</a>, and
        issues and PRs are always welcome.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: 1040px;
  }

  .page-head h1 {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 2rem;
    max-width: 16ch;
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .prose h2 {
    margin: 3.2rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .dropcap::first-letter {
    float: left;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 4.6em;
    line-height: 0.8;
    padding: 0.08em 0.12em 0 0;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .pull {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding: 0 0 0 1.4rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--award);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3vw, 2.3rem);
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .vocab-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0 0 1.2rem;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .vocab-list li {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .soul {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0.9rem;
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .soul-card {
    padding: 1.4rem 1.4rem 1.2rem;
  }

  .soul-card h3 {
    margin: 0.8rem 0 0.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 1.9rem;
    line-height: 1.05;
  }

  .soul-challenges h3 {
    color: var(--danger);
  }

  .soul-card p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.93rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .principles {
    padding-left: 1.3rem;
  }

  .principles li {
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
    padding-left: 0.3rem;
  }

  .principles li::marker {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .contribute {
    margin-top: 2.4rem;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .soul {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
